WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.283
Welcome to Lecture 4 of the

2
00:00:02.356 --> 00:00:04.780
FrontierCS 2 course, on networks.

3
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.662
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

4
00:00:07.732 --> 00:00:09.490
removes its key idea from

5
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.107
the authors' own code

6
00:00:11.107 --> 00:00:13.850
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

7
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.719
The agent passes only if it beats the

8
00:00:16.787 --> 00:00:18.830
authors' code on every metric

9
00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.600
on every hidden workload.

10
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.910
This lecture covers three such tasks.

11
00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.430
We start with BBQ.

12
00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:27.918
A network card has to decide

13
00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:29.662
which packet to send next

14
00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:31.470
millions of times a second.

15
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.848
Each packet has a priority

16
00:00:33.848 --> 00:00:35.500
and zero is the best.

17
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.791
BBQ, from NSDI 2024

18
00:00:37.791 --> 00:00:41.770
builds this priority queue as a circuit.

19
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.200
Take sixteen priorities.

20
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.750
BBQ keeps them in a tree of small bitmaps.

21
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.247
The bottom level has four rows of four bits

22
00:00:51.247 --> 00:00:52.820
one bit per priority.

23
00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.926
The top level has one row

24
00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:56.884
its bit says whether the row

25
00:00:56.952 --> 00:00:58.370
below holds anything.

26
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.531
And every bit has a counter of

27
00:01:00.591 --> 00:01:02.160
the packets underneath it.

28
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.750
Here the queue holds two packets

29
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.410
of priority one, one of priority two

30
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.230
and two of priority nine.

31
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.521
To remove the best packet

32
00:01:11.521 --> 00:01:13.890
follow the first one at each level.

33
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.333
At the top, the first one is bit zero

34
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:18.880
so go to row zero.

35
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.445
In row zero, the first one is bit one

36
00:01:22.445 --> 00:01:23.630
priority one.

37
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.725
Then subtract one from each

38
00:01:25.789 --> 00:01:27.340
counter on the way down.

39
00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.929
The first removal changes no bit

40
00:01:29.997 --> 00:01:31.761
because priority one still

41
00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:33.050
has a packet left.

42
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.004
The second empties it

43
00:01:35.004 --> 00:01:36.680
and its bit turns off.

44
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.875
The third takes priority two

45
00:01:38.941 --> 00:01:40.654
and empties the whole row

46
00:01:40.654 --> 00:01:42.630
so the top bit turns off too.

47
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.340
Here is the catch.

48
00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.534
Each removal must know whether

49
00:01:46.596 --> 00:01:48.194
a count just reached zero

50
00:01:48.256 --> 00:01:49.977
and the next removal arrives

51
00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:51.330
one clock tick later.

52
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.591
That back-to-back dependency is

53
00:01:53.652 --> 00:01:55.440
what the whole task is about.

54
00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.607
The task removes the logic

55
00:01:57.668 --> 00:01:58.828
of the tree levels

56
00:01:58.828 --> 00:02:00.660
and the agent writes it back.

57
00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.102
The judge then times every design

58
00:02:03.166 --> 00:02:04.370
with a fixed model.

59
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.786
Logic is cut into lookup tables

60
00:02:06.786 --> 00:02:09.304
each costing five hundred picoseconds

61
00:02:09.304 --> 00:02:12.080
plus three hundred for every clock period.

62
00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.052
A stretch that starts right after a memory

63
00:02:15.115 --> 00:02:17.550
read costs a thousand picoseconds more.

64
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.472
So three lookup tables after a register take

65
00:02:20.531 --> 00:02:22.811
one thousand eight hundred picoseconds

66
00:02:22.869 --> 00:02:25.091
and the same three after a memory read

67
00:02:25.149 --> 00:02:27.020
take two thousand eight hundred.

68
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.479
The authors keep their counters

69
00:02:29.539 --> 00:02:30.570
in memory blocks

70
00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.449
which pack thousands of values

71
00:02:32.509 --> 00:02:33.600
into little space.

72
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:35.644
But a memory read lands late

73
00:02:35.705 --> 00:02:36.854
in its clock cycle

74
00:02:36.915 --> 00:02:39.032
and by then an operation just ahead

75
00:02:39.093 --> 00:02:41.150
may have changed the same counter.

76
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.717
So after every read

77
00:02:42.777 --> 00:02:45.150
the reference compares the counter with

78
00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.157
what the operations ahead wrote

79
00:02:47.157 --> 00:02:48.860
and keeps the newest value.

80
00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.612
That comparison is the slowest stretch

81
00:02:51.674 --> 00:02:54.015
two thousand eight hundred picoseconds

82
00:02:54.076 --> 00:02:55.370
on the smaller trees.

83
00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.683
Pause here and predict.

84
00:02:57.683 --> 00:02:59.039
Which helps more

85
00:02:59.039 --> 00:03:02.128
one lookup table fewer after the memory

86
00:03:02.128 --> 00:03:03.710
or no memory at all?

87
00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.034
One fewer gives two thousand

88
00:03:09.097 --> 00:03:10.744
three hundred picoseconds.

89
00:03:10.744 --> 00:03:13.532
No memory gives one thousand eight hundred

90
00:03:13.532 --> 00:03:15.306
the floor for this circuit.

91
00:03:15.369 --> 00:03:16.890
Two agents found the two

92
00:03:16.953 --> 00:03:18.220
different ways down.

93
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.190
Gemini 4 Argon, running in Pi

94
00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.020
kept every memory.

95
00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.698
It noticed that which value is newest

96
00:03:26.761 --> 00:03:29.027
does not depend on the value itself

97
00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:30.977
only on which operations ahead

98
00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.614
touched the same counter

99
00:03:32.614 --> 00:03:34.690
and registers already know that.

100
00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.389
So it makes the choice during the read

101
00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.955
and only one lookup table

102
00:03:39.016 --> 00:03:40.160
follows the memory.

103
00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.350
Claude Opus 5.5 removed the memory instead.

104
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.893
Each bucket gets three registers

105
00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.484
so a level needs one stage, not three

106
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.278
and there is never a newer

107
00:03:51.344 --> 00:03:52.540
value to look for.

108
00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.473
The circuit from Gemini runs one point two

109
00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:58.608
two to one point five six times as many operations

110
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.330
per second as the authors'.

111
00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.082
Opus's runs one point five six to one

112
00:04:03.147 --> 00:04:05.160
point eight four times as many.

113
00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:07.420
Speed was not free.

114
00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.338
At four thousand ninety-six priorities

115
00:04:10.404 --> 00:04:12.906
Opus's design uses about sixteen times

116
00:04:12.972 --> 00:04:15.210
the lookup tables of the authors'.

117
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.779
And the hidden workloads were wider than

118
00:04:17.834 --> 00:04:19.720
anything the agents could test on.

119
00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.897
GPT-6 Astra reached the floor

120
00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.661
on every tree it was given

121
00:04:23.724 --> 00:04:26.433
but at width sixteen its clock rose to four

122
00:04:26.496 --> 00:04:28.700
thousand eight hundred picoseconds

123
00:04:28.763 --> 00:04:30.653
slower than the authors' three

124
00:04:30.716 --> 00:04:33.110
thousand eight hundred, and it failed.

125
00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.490
Five of sixteen models passed BBQ.

126
00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.730
The best score was forty-two point two

127
00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.212
by Claude Opus 5.5

128
00:04:41.212 --> 00:04:43.880
against twenty-six for the authors.

129
00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:45.830
Three things to take away.

130
00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.460
A bit is one when its counter is above zero.

131
00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.777
The clock is set by the slowest

132
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.490
stretch between registers.

133
00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.943
And a design must hold up beyond

134
00:04:56.009 --> 00:04:57.520
the cases you can test.

135
00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:58.846
As an exercise

136
00:04:58.911 --> 00:05:00.928
write the dequeue for the worst

137
00:05:00.993 --> 00:05:02.750
packet instead of the best.

138
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.940
Next, MetaOpt.
